The banner image itself can’t hold clickable links, so the links need to go in your LinkedIn profile. These are the best places, with the exact steps.

1. Featured section (most visible)

Go to your profile and scroll to Featured. If you don’t see it, click Add profile section, then Recommended, then Add featured.
Click + and choose Add a link.
Paste the URL, add a title such as “Python DSA Platform (live demo)” and a short description, then save.
Repeat for PG Finder and for your GitHub profile.

Your lnkd.in/... links are LinkedIn’s own shortened links, so you can paste them as they are.

2. Contact info (clickable website links)

Click Contact info under your name on your profile.
Click the pencil icon, then under Website choose Add website.
Paste the URL and pick a label like “Portfolio” or “Other”. You can add a few.

LinkedIn may show only the label you chose, not the full URL. Add a custom label such as “GitHub” or “PG Finder” so it’s clear.

3. Projects section (best for describing each project)

Click Add profile section, then Recommended, then Add projects.
Enter the project name, dates, a short description, and your role. For Jobnex, you can mark that you led the team.
Paste the deployment URL in the Project URL field and save.

4. About section
Paste the links as plain text at the end of your About, for example “GitHub: github.com/yourusername”. LinkedIn makes them clickable automatically.

GitHub URL
Your GitHub profile link is https://github.com/ followed by your username. You can find it by opening your GitHub profile and copying the address bar. If you send me your username, I can add github.com/yourusername to the banner as plain text so people know where to look.

I’d start with Featured and the Projects section, since they give recruiters one click to your work.
